{"schema":"cast.script/3","book":{"title":"83_The_Laughing_Willow","source":"83_The_Laughing_Willow.epub","spans":1747,"chapters":5,"accent":"en-US","person":"first"},"narrator":{"gender":"unknown","self":null},"quality":{"dialogue_spans":107,"attributed":6,"attribution_rate":0.0561,"voiced":4,"voiced_rate":0.0374,"confident_min":0.6,"needs_review_count":101,"needs_review":[204,218,251,265,303,305,307,309,311,314,388,495,497,502,505,507,511,513,516,518,522,524,528,530,534,536,710,765,767,769,771,929,950,952,956,960,964,968,983,992,993,1018,1020,1022,1049,1058,1060,1067,1079,1140,1142,1154,1156,1160,1165,1195,1198,1201,1207,1211,1213,1218,1226,1228,1231,1236,1237,1284,1291,1301,1313,1315,1320,1322,1332,1334,1337,1338,1347,1350,1376,1381,1390,1392,1398,1400,1416,1426,1434,1439,1478,1507,1509,1539,1541,1561,1563,1570,1572,1641,1645]},"cast":[{"name":"Spring","voice":"principal_1","lines":2,"gender":"female","age":"unknown","hint":"neutral"},{"name":"Molly","voice":"principal_2","lines":1,"gender":"female","age":"unknown","hint":"name"},{"name":"Father Wilhelm","voice":"principal_3","lines":1,"gender":"male","age":"unknown","hint":"title"},{"name":"Robert Rice","voice":"narrator","lines":2,"gender":"male","age":"unknown","hint":"name"}],"policy":{"percentile":0.88,"min_chunks":2,"min_gap":8,"max_coverage":0.12},"music":{"chunks":138,"coverage":0.0652,"cues":[]},"chapters":[{"title":"Introduction: Wit in Wartime","start":0},{"title":"THE LAUGHING WILLOW","start":10},{"title":"Notes on the Text","start":1696},{"title":"A Reader's Guide","start":1720},{"title":"Enjoyed this edition?","start":1734}],"free":{"until_span":100,"chapters":0,"reason":"capped"},"spans":[{"i":0,"kind":"narration","text":"The Laughing Willow was published in New York by George H. Doran in 1918, in the last year of the Great War, and it is not the book that its gentle title leads you to expect. Roughly the first third of it is war satire, aimed squarely and repeatedly at the German Emperor and his son. Readers who come to Oliver Herford expecting only elves and dormice should know that before they begin.","voice":"narrator"},{"i":1,"kind":"narration","text":"Herford by then was in his late fifties and had spent thirty years as one of the most reliably funny men in American magazines. He had built his reputation on small comic poems accompanied by his own pen-and-ink drawings, on epigrams, and on a light touch that never seemed to cost him anything. What this book records is what happened when that touch was turned on a world war.","voice":"narrator"},{"i":2,"kind":"narration","text":"The result is stranger and better than propaganda. Herford does not write hatred. He writes contempt, which is a different weapon and in some ways a crueler one. Kaiser Wilhelm II appears here as Father Wilhelm, a doddering old man being interviewed by his son to the tune of Lewis Carroll, and as Billi Kaisam, Surnamed the Tentbreaker, presiding over a parody of the Rubaiyat. The Crown Prince appears as Crownie, whose famous coolness under fire turns out to be the temperature of a cigar smoked safely behind the lines. The technique throughout is to deny these men their scale. An enemy you fear is dangerous. An enemy who balances his crown on the end of his nose is finished.","voice":"narrator"},{"i":3,"kind":"narration","text":"Around the war verse Herford builds the rest of a working humorist's year. There are epitaphs, animal poems of the kind he had been writing since the 1880s, a sequence called About People I Have Met that includes J. M. Barrie and Mark Twain, a companion sequence of People I Have Not Met, society satire in which the Devil opens a Manhattan dress shop to teach fashionable women how to defraud the customs service, and a set of poems built on quotations Herford found in magazines and could not resist. The book is a magazine humorist's shelf swept into covers, and its unevenness is part of its honesty.","voice":"narrator"},{"i":4,"kind":"narration","text":"The dedication, To Peg, rewrites Robert Burns. Where Burns wished for the gift to see ourselves as others see us, Herford wishes it on Peg and then immediately concludes that the giver would want it back. That is the register of the whole book. Even the affection arrives with a small blade in it.","voice":"narrator"},{"i":5,"kind":"narration","text":"A century on, the war poems have become something their author did not intend. They are a record of what it felt like to be told, week after week, that the correct response to Wilhelm II was laughter. Some of the jokes have dated into obscurity and are glossed in the Notes below. Others land exactly as hard as they did in 1918, because the technique of shrinking a tyrant by describing his wardrobe has not aged at all.","voice":"narrator"},{"i":6,"kind":"narration","text":"This Carrier Press edition restores the complete text with Herford's original drawings, and adds an Introduction, Notes on the Text explaining the wartime and topical references, and a Reader's Guide. A note on audience: this is a book of adult light verse and topical satire. It is not a children's book, and the war material in particular assumes an adult reader.","voice":"narrator"},{"i":7,"kind":"narration","text":"Jeffrey L. Carrier Carrier Press","voice":"narrator"},{"i":8,"kind":"narration","text":"TRANSCRIBER’S NOTE","voice":"narrator"},{"i":9,"kind":"narration","text":"Some minor changes are noted at the end of the book.","voice":"narrator"},{"i":10,"kind":"narration","text":"OLIVER HERFORD","voice":"narrator"},{"i":11,"kind":"narration","text":"Apropos de Rien","voice":"narrator"},{"i":12,"kind":"narration","text":"THE","voice":"narrator"},{"i":13,"kind":"narration","text":"LAUGHING WILLOW","voice":"narrator"},{"i":14,"kind":"narration","text":"VERSES AND PICTURES","voice":"narrator"},{"i":15,"kind":"narration","text":"BY","voice":"narrator"},{"i":16,"kind":"narration","text":"OLIVER HERFORD","voice":"narrator"},{"i":17,"kind":"narration","text":"Author of","voice":"narrator"},{"i":18,"kind":"narration","text":"“Artful Antics,”","voice":"narrator"},{"i":19,"kind":"narration","text":"“The Child’s Primer of Natural History,”","voice":"narrator"},{"i":20,"kind":"narration","text":"“Overheard in a Garden,”","voice":"narrator"},{"i":21,"kind":"narration","text":"“Fairy Godmother-in-Law,”","voice":"narrator"},{"i":22,"kind":"narration","text":"“Astonishing Tale of a Pen and Ink Puppet,”","voice":"narrator"},{"i":23,"kind":"narration","text":"“The Confessions of a Caricaturist,”","voice":"narrator"},{"i":24,"kind":"narration","text":"etc.","voice":"narrator"},{"i":25,"kind":"narration","text":"NEW YORK GEORGE H. DORAN COMPANY","voice":"narrator"},{"i":26,"kind":"narration","text":"TO PEG","voice":"narrator"},{"i":27,"kind":"narration","text":"Oh, should some power the giftie gie her","voice":"narrator"},{"i":28,"kind":"narration","text":"To see hersel’ as ithers see her,","voice":"narrator"},{"i":29,"kind":"narration","text":"I’m thinking Peg would grow sae vain","voice":"narrator"},{"i":30,"kind":"narration","text":"He’d take the giftie back again.","voice":"narrator"},{"i":31,"kind":"narration","text":"CONTENTS","voice":"narrator"},{"i":32,"kind":"narration","text":"PAGE","voice":"narrator"},{"i":33,"kind":"narration","text":"Epitaphs","voice":"narrator"},{"i":34,"kind":"narration","text":"9","voice":"narrator"},{"i":35,"kind":"narration","text":"The Truth About Russia","voice":"narrator"},{"i":36,"kind":"narration","text":"11","voice":"narrator"},{"i":37,"kind":"narration","text":"The Wedding Feast","voice":"narrator"},{"i":38,"kind":"narration","text":"11","voice":"narrator"},{"i":39,"kind":"narration","text":"A Mujik","voice":"narrator"},{"i":40,"kind":"narration","text":"12","voice":"narrator"},{"i":41,"kind":"narration","text":"The Cossack","voice":"narrator"},{"i":42,"kind":"narration","text":"13","voice":"narrator"},{"i":43,"kind":"narration","text":"The Three S’s","voice":"narrator"},{"i":44,"kind":"narrati